**定理 82.1（one-leaf obstructions and endpoint）。** For L>1,d0<=1 take t=beta and the single preparation (e1/r,r e2), 1/L<r<1. Then Delta=1, |a|=1/r<L, |b|=r<1; the ORIGINAL three reads are (r e2,-e3,r e1), hence q0=q2=r,q1=1 and q_j=r^F_(j-1). At r=1 the unit neutral member is also admissible. Thus decay scale is unbounded at N=1, including d0=1. At L=1 with standing d0<1, t=alpha, preparation (lambda e1,lambda e2), d0^(1/4)<=lambda<1, gives q_j=lambda^F_(j+1), Delta=lambda^4, and its neutral limit lambda=1. No unit neutral member is used on d0>1. The one-leaf witnesses already establish these fixed-size claims and the equality d0=1 case.

For 0<E<1/2 the same geometric rate packing in the one-leaf family gives arbitrarily many mutually >2E separated trajectories; thus no finite cap depending only on N=1 exists. For E=1/2 use an independent FINITE-ALPHABET argument, not strict packing and not countable-word pigeonhole. For each unit direction u choose rotated versions of a contracting source sequence whose fixed-j first phase converges to u. A finite alphabet assigns one word infinitely often in each direction's countable sequence. For two directions assigned the same recurrent word, its fixed-j outputs lie within E of both limiting unit vectors for every j in that phase. Select one particular contracting source assigned that word; along that phase its future tends to zero. Compactness then gives an accumulation center within E of 0,u,v. A radius-1/2 ball containing 0 and a unit u has the unique center u/2, so v must equal u. Finitely many words cannot cover the sphere. This proves the endpoint obstruction already at N=1 on the neutral-containing plateaus.

The same FINITE-ALPHABET proof uses the actual constant-Delta family as k->infinity on the neutral-free plateau, with all tree sizes allowed. The limiting unit vectors need not themselves be actual sources in this domain. It follows that C_N(1/2)->infinity by monotonicity: a uniform cap over all N would cover their union with one finite word universe. The finite core gives C_N(1/2)=O(loglog(N+2)); no matched endpoint rate is supplied. The matched endpoint rate remains unresolved.

A countable-word recurrence inference on a countable source sequence is false: the countable sequence indexed by k can use the unique finite binary code of k. No word has to recur infinitely. Consequently variable-word impossibility, source-dependent-tolerance prohibition, and endpoint conclusions resting only on that inference do not follow. The independently proved finite-alphabet endpoint is kept. Every plateau source individually has a finite source-aware word for every fixed positive E: a contracting source uses the finite core, and a neutral source has a finite periodic direction approximation. Thus variable word length is a real change of scope.

Acquisition by arbitrary fine names is not total at neutral sources for E<=1/2. Suppose it terminates on an exact unit-axis neutral name. Its finite queried precisions and replies have positive error slack. Choose small opposite rotations +/-theta and contracting one-leaf sources approaching that neutral preparation; for every source sufficiently close, the entire finite adaptive transcript can be the same valid replies, and it extends to a valid fine name. A closed word from that transcript must cover one fixed contracting source and both sequences approaching the rotated unit limits. In one phase it would therefore give a ball containing 0,u_+,u_-. For 0<2theta<pi/2 the minimum enclosing radius is 1/(2cos theta)>1/2, a contradiction. This is a finite-prefix argument for the actual source, not a countability argument. A fixed nonzero noise floor allows the same common offimage reports and cannot replace arbitrarily fine names. At d0>1 the fixed-j unit limits from large legal trees yield the analogous original finite-reply obstruction; the paid pointwise-total encoder works because no actual source is neutral, while its required precision is not uniformly bounded over all sizes.

For $\|u\|=\|v\|=1$ with angle $\theta\in[0,\pi]$, the minimum enclosing radius of $\{0,u,v\}$ is

$$
\operatorname{rad}\{0,u,v\}=\begin{cases}
1/(2\cos(\theta/2)),&0\leq\theta\leq\pi/2,\\
\sin(\theta/2),&\pi/2\leq\theta\leq\pi.
\end{cases}
$$

For an acute triangle the circumcenter on the angle bisector balances all three distances. For an obtuse angle the midpoint of the longest edge $[u,v]$ has radius $\sin(\theta/2)$ and includes zero since $\cos(\theta/2)\leq\sin(\theta/2)$; half the longest-edge length is also a lower bound. At equality the formulae agree, and the degenerate endpoints follow by the segment bound. The circumradius expression does not extend into the obtuse range.

